Two separate problems. The `gcloud secrets list` failure is a CLI quirk with regional secrets. The empty `grep` means the secret names aren't written literally in `dist`, probably because the app builds them dynamically. Read the app's own loader instead, since that is the source of truth:

```bash
cd /var/www/careernavigator-backend
grep -rlE "secretmanager|accessSecretVersion|SecretManager" dist --include=*.js | head
```
Then open the file it lists first, masking nothing, since the file should only contain names and no values:
```bash
grep -nE "secret|locations|projects|DB_|process\.env" <file-from-above>
```

**In parallel, list the secrets two ways** (read-only). Try the first. If it still errors, use the second:
```bash
gcloud beta secrets list --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 --location=me-central2 --format="value(name)"
```
```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)" "https://secretmanager.me-central2.rep.googleapis.com/v1/projects/pg-ae-n-app-125609/locations/me-central2/secrets" | grep '"name"'
```
A `403` is expected here and is a good sign. The VM account `sa-cn-backend` should have only `secretAccessor`, not list permission. In that case the loader code from the first command tells us the names, and we skip listing.

Paste the outputs of the first and third commands at minimum. Don't paste secret values.
